          A 3500 watt generator is an essentials machine with a 120 volt ceiling. It runs a refrigerator, a gas furnace blower, lights, the internet and a few small appliances in an outage, and it runs a 30-amp travel trailer comfortably as long as the rooftop air conditioner is either off or soft-started. It does not run anything on 240 volts, it will not carry two big heating loads at once, and it trips more often from habits than from undersizing. This page lays out the working budget, three outage plans, the RV numbers, and the short list of things that trip it.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="the-budget" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The 3,500 Watt Budget: Running vs Starting
          </h2>

          <p>
            Generators sold as 3500 watt models are rated for roughly 3,000 to 3,600 running watts and somewhere between about 3,500 and 4,650 starting watts, depending on whether they are open-frame or inverter designs. The running number is what the generator carries for hours. The starting number is a burst of a second or two for a motor that is spinning up. Plan every list around the running number on the fuel you will actually use, because dual fuel units typically lose around 10 percent on propane.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then take 80 percent of it. Keeping the continuous load to roughly 80 percent of the running rating is common guidance from generator makers and electricians, and it is the difference between a generator that runs all night and one that trips at 3 a.m. when the refrigerator and furnace happen to cycle together. On a unit rated 3,500 running watts, the working budget is about 2,800 watts. On a 3,000 running watt inverter, it is about 2,400. That is the number to plan against.
          </p>

          <p>
            The starting side is checked separately. Add up everything that will be running, then add the starting watts of the single biggest motor that might kick on. If that total fits under the starting rating, the list works. At 3,500 watts, the gap between running and starting is often only 600 to 1,100 watts, which is why the biggest motor on your list, usually the refrigerator, sump pump or RV air conditioner, decides what else you can run alongside it.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is also a per-outlet limit that bigger generators hide. The household-style duplex outlets on a 3500 watt generator are typically 20 amp 5-20R receptacles, each behind its own 20 amp breaker, which is 2,400 watts per duplex. If you plug a 1,500 watt space heater and a refrigerator into the same duplex, that outlet's breaker can trip with the generator nowhere near full. Spread loads across outlets, and use the 30 amp outlet, if the generator has one, for the heaviest cord.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The 3,500 Watt Class in Four Numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">~2,800W</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">80% working budget on 3,500W</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">2,400W</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Per 20A duplex outlet</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">3,600W</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">TT-30 RV outlet ceiling</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">120V</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Only voltage most units offer</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            All the appliance figures below are typical ranges from our <a href="/generator-wattage-chart/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">generator wattage chart</a>, not measurements of your appliances. Read the labels, or use a plug-in watt meter on anything with a cord, and swap your own numbers in.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="outage-plans" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Three Essentials-Only Outage Plans
          </h2>

          <p>
            A 3500 watt generator works in an outage when you decide in advance what it is for. The three plans below cover the common households. Each fits inside the 2,800 watt working budget of a 3,500 running watt unit; none of them includes air conditioning, electric heat or electric cooking, because the generator cannot carry those alongside anything else.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Plan A: An apartment, condo or small house with no basement</h3>

          <p>
            Refrigerator at about 700 watts, lights, router, a laptop and phone chargers at about 300, a television at 100 to 200, and a CPAP machine, which typically draws well under 100 watts without its humidifier. The running total is around 1,200 watts. The worst start is the refrigerator compressor at about 2,200 with 500 watts of other loads running, or 2,700. That leaves room for a microwave or coffee maker on its own, and even a 1,500 watt space heater for one room, though that triples fuel use. Honestly, if this is your whole list, a 3500 watt unit is more than you need; a <a href="/best-2000-watt-inverter-generator/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">2000 watt inverter</a> is quieter and lighter for it.
          </p>

          <p>
            The catch for apartment dwellers is legal and physical, not electrical. A generator must run outdoors, well away from doors, windows and vents, and many buildings forbid running one on a balcony at all. If you cannot put it at least 20 feet from the building, a battery power station is the safer fit for this list.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Plan B: A house with gas heat and no sump pump</h3>

          <p>
            Refrigerator at 700, chest freezer at 500, gas furnace blower and controls at 600 to 900, and lights, router and chargers at 400. The running total is 2,200 to 2,500 watts, inside the budget. The worst start is the refrigerator or furnace blower, around 2,200 to 2,400, with the rest running at roughly 1,800, or about 4,000 to 4,200. That fits under the 4,375 to 4,650 starting watts most open-frame 3,500 watt units are rated for, but not under a 3,000 running watt inverter with a lower surge figure. The furnace is hardwired, so it needs a transfer switch or a dedicated generator-ready connection.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Plan C: A house that needs the sump pump</h3>

          <p>
            Add a 1/2 HP sump pump, which runs at 800 to 1,050 watts and starts at 2,000 to 2,600, and the list no longer fits with everything on. The fix is to drop the freezer and let the pump own the starting headroom: refrigerator, furnace, lights and sump pump run at about 2,800 watts, and the sump start lands around 4,400 to 4,800 in the worst case, right on the line. Our <a href="/best-3500-watt-generator/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">3500 watt generator guide</a> works through that overlap with specific models; the short version is that a 1/2 HP pump is the most a 3,500 watt unit should be asked to carry.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Plan</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Running total</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Worst-case start</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Fits a 3,500W unit?</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">A: Apartment or condo</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~1,200W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~2,700W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, with room to spare</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">B: Gas heat, no sump</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2,200-2,500W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~4,000-4,200W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes on open-frame; tight on smaller inverters</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">C: Gas heat plus 1/2 HP sump</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~2,800W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~4,400-4,800W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Only without the freezer, and only just</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="rv-no-ac" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. A 30-Amp RV Without the Air Conditioner
          </h2>

          <p>
            A 30-amp RV plugs into a TT-30R receptacle, a 120 volt, 30 amp outlet. Thirty amps at 120 volts is 3,600 watts, so a 3,500 watt generator with a TT-30R can supply nearly everything the RV's main breaker is designed to pass. That is why this size is so popular with travel trailer owners: the cord plugs straight in, and the generator and the rig are close to matched.
          </p>

          <p>
            With the air conditioner off, the RV's loads are modest but bursty. The converter that charges the house batteries typically draws a few hundred watts, rising toward 900 or so when a large converter is recharging a flat bank. An absorption refrigerator on electric mode runs at roughly 300 to 400 watts. The electric element in a 6 or 10 gallon water heater is commonly around 1,400 watts. The water pump, furnace blower and lights mostly run on 12V through the converter. A microwave rated at 1,000 cooking watts typically pulls 1,400 to 1,500 from the outlet, and a hair dryer 1,500 to 1,800.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">RV load (no AC)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical watts</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Notes</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Converter / battery charger</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">200-900</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Highest when batteries are low</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Absorption fridge, electric mode</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">300-400</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Switch to propane to free the watts</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Water heater electric element</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~1,400</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Switch to propane to free the watts</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Microwave, 1,000W cooking</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,400-1,500</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Short, heavy, resistive</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Hair dryer or electric kettle</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,200-1,800</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">The usual trip culprit</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Lights, TV, fans, phones</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">100-300</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mostly 12V via the converter</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Add them up and the RV can ask for 5,000 watts or more if everything resistive runs at once, which is how people trip a generator that is perfectly sized. In practice nobody needs the water heater element, the microwave and a hair dryer in the same minute. Run the fridge and water heater on propane whenever the generator is the power source, and the electric budget is left for the converter, the microwave and the electronics, which sit comfortably inside 2,800 watts.
          </p>

          <p>
            For dry camping without air conditioning, a 3,500 watt unit is generous. Many owners find a 2,000 to 2,500 watt inverter does the same job more quietly if they never plan to run the AC; our <a href="/best-generator-for-camping/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">camping generator picks</a> and <a href="/best-1500-watt-generator/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">1500 watt generator guide</a> cover the smaller end. The reason to carry 3,500 watts is the next section.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="rv-rooftop-ac" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The 13,500 BTU Rooftop AC, With and Without Soft Start
          </h2>

          <p>
            The rooftop air conditioner is the load that decides whether a 3500 watt generator suits your RV. A 13,500 BTU unit, the most common size on 30-amp trailers, typically runs at about 1,500 to 1,800 watts once it is cooling, more on a hot afternoon. Its compressor start is the problem: it can surge well past 3,000 watts for a fraction of a second, and in high heat or at altitude the surge is larger and the generator weaker.
          </p>

          <p>
            Without a soft starter, an open-frame 3,500 watt generator rated around 4,375 to 4,650 starting watts will usually start a 13,500 BTU unit if little else is running. Switch the fridge and water heater to propane, leave the microwave off, and let the converter finish its bulk charge before starting the AC. An inverter generator with 3,000 to 3,500 running watts and a smaller surge rating is much more hit and miss. Owners commonly report that the AC starts on a cool morning and trips the generator on a hot afternoon, because inverter overload protection reacts in milliseconds.
          </p>

          <p>
            A soft starter changes that. It is a small module wired into the air conditioner that ramps the compressor up over a fraction of a second instead of slamming it on, and soft start makers advertise cuts in starting current of well over half. Owners commonly report that a soft-started 13,500 BTU unit starts reliably on generators that could not start it before, including smaller inverters. The installation is a common DIY job on RV rooftop units, though it involves opening the AC shroud and working on its wiring, so follow the kit's instructions or have an RV technician fit it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Once the AC is running, the arithmetic is tight but workable. A 1,700 watt AC leaves about 1,100 watts of the 2,800 watt working budget, enough for the converter, lights, a television and phones. It is not enough for the microwave at the same time. The habit that makes a 3500 watt generator work with AC is simple: switch the AC off for the five minutes the microwave runs, then switch it back on. A 15,000 BTU unit runs closer to 2,000 watts or more and leaves almost nothing, and two air conditioners on a 50-amp rig are out of reach for this class entirely.
          </p>

          <p>
            Altitude and heat derate the generator itself. A commonly quoted rule of thumb is about 3.5 percent of output lost per 1,000 feet of elevation, so at 5,000 feet a 3,500 watt generator behaves more like a 2,900 watt one. If you camp in the mountains or the desert in summer, fit the soft starter, or move up to a <a href="/best-4000-watt-generator/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">4000 watt generator</a>. Our <a href="/best-rv-generator/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">RV generator guide</a> covers 50-amp rigs and parallel kits.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RV RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">AC on, microwave off. Fridge and water heater on propane.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Those three habits are the whole difference between a 3500 watt generator that runs a 30-amp RV all afternoon and one that trips every time the compressor cycles. A soft starter on the AC turns a marginal setup into a reliable one.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="what-trips-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. What Trips a 3500 Watt Generator
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most trips at this size are not because the generator is too small for the job. They come from a handful of predictable situations, and each has an easy fix once you know what it is.
          </p>

          <p>
            Stacked resistive loads are the most common. A coffee maker at up to 1,200 watts, a toaster or microwave at 1,000 to 1,500, a hair dryer at 1,500 to 1,800, an electric kettle or a space heater at 1,500: any two of these together, plus a running refrigerator, push a 3,500 watt generator past its running rating. They draw their full rating continuously, with no surge to warn you. The rule is one heating appliance at a time, and none at all while a big motor is starting.
          </p>

          <p>
            Simultaneous motor starts are the second. After the generator is started or reset with everything plugged in, the refrigerator, freezer, furnace blower and sump pump may all try to start in the same second. Two starts of around 2,200 watts each, on top of anything else running, is past every 3,500 watt unit's surge rating. Start the generator with nothing connected, let it warm up, then add loads one at a time with the biggest motor first, waiting for each to settle.
          </p>

          <p>
            The third is an outlet breaker, not the main one. If one duplex trips while the generator is lightly loaded, you have exceeded that outlet's 20 amps, typically by running two heavy loads through one power strip or cord. Move one of them to another outlet. Long, thin extension cords cause a related problem: voltage drop makes motors draw more current to start, so a refrigerator on 100 feet of 16 gauge cord can trip a generator it would start easily on 25 feet of 12 gauge. Our <a href="/generator-extension-cord-size/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">generator extension cord guide</a> has the gauge-by-length table.
          </p>

          <p>
            The last group is the generator losing output while the load stays the same: running on propane, at altitude, in high heat, or with a dirty air filter or stale fuel. Each takes a few percent off, and together they can turn a 3,500 watt generator into a 2,800 watt one. If a list that worked in spring trips in August, that is usually why. Trim the list rather than fighting it, and see our guide to <a href="/generator-runs-but-no-power/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">a generator that runs but gives no power</a> if the problem is the opposite: no output at all.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="the-120v-wall" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. The 120V-Only Wall: What It Can't Run at All
          </h2>

          <p>
            Almost every generator in the 3,500 watt class is 120 volt only. Its 30 amp outlet, if it has one, is a TT-30R RV outlet or an L5-30R twist-lock, both 120V. There is no L14-30R, the four-prong 120/240V outlet that larger home backup generators use. That single fact rules out a list of household loads regardless of how many watts they draw.
          </p>

          <p>
            The 240V loads it cannot run include most well pumps, electric water heaters, electric ranges and ovens, electric clothes dryers, central air conditioners and heat pumps, electric furnaces and baseboard heaters, and Level 2 EV chargers. Several of these would be too big anyway, but the well pump is the painful one: a 1/2 HP or 3/4 HP 240V pump is small enough in watts that people expect a 3,500 watt generator to handle it, and it cannot. Our guide to <a href="/what-size-generator-runs-a-well-pump/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">what size generator runs a well pump</a> covers what does.
          </p>

          <p>
            The 120V limit also changes how you connect to the house. A transfer switch fed from a 120V-only generator can only energise one leg of your panel, so only the circuits on that leg get power. Some installers bridge both legs together so a 120V generator feeds every selected circuit. That can overload a shared neutral on a multi-wire branch circuit, a common arrangement in kitchens and older houses, so it is a decision for a licensed electrician who has checked your panel, not a DIY trick. Never back-feed a panel through a dryer or wall outlet with a double-ended cord; it can energise the utility lines.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most 3,500 watt owners, the honest setup is cords, not panel wiring. Run a heavy-duty cord to the refrigerator and freezer, a power strip for the electronics, and leave the hardwired loads alone. The exception is the gas furnace blower: a licensed electrician can fit a generator-ready inlet or a single-circuit transfer switch to the furnace circuit so the 120V generator can run it safely. That is often the best few hundred dollars a 3,500 watt owner spends.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want the hardwired loads covered properly, you need 240V. That starts in the 5,000 watt class with an L14-30R outlet; our page on <a href="/what-will-a-5000-watt-generator-run/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">what a 5000 watt generator will run</a> does the same arithmetic one size up, including a 240V well pump, and the <a href="/best-240v-generator/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">240V generator guide</a> covers the outlet types.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="right-size" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. When 3,500 Watts Is the Wrong Size
          </h2>

          <p>
            The 3,500 watt class is the right size for a narrow but very common set of people: a household with gas heat that wants the food cold and the lights on, with at most a 1/2 HP sump pump, and a 30-amp RV owner who either camps without the AC or will fit a soft starter. For those buyers, it is the cheapest, lightest generator that does the whole job, and the extra cost of going bigger buys nothing they will use.
          </p>

          <p>
            It is too big if your list is a refrigerator, lights and phones, or a weekend of campsite power without air conditioning. A 2,000 to 2,500 watt inverter does that more quietly, on less fuel, at half the weight, and you will actually take it camping. Owners who buy 3,500 watts "just in case" often report that the size and noise mean it stays in the shed.
          </p>

          <p>
            It is too small if any of these are true: you have a well; your sump pump is 3/4 HP or bigger; you want a window air conditioner and a freezer and a sump pump at the same time; your RV is 50-amp or has two air conditioners; or you want your furnace, sump pump and some lighting circuits on a proper transfer switch. Each of those points to the 5,000 to 6,500 watt range with a 240V outlet.
          </p>

          <p>
            It is the wrong tool entirely if the goal is central air, electric cooking or the whole house running as normal. That is 10,000 watts and up, or a standby generator, and no amount of load management closes the gap. Our <a href="/what-size-generator-do-i-need-for-my-house/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">house generator sizing guide</a> walks through the full list if you are not sure where you land.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are in the sweet spot, the remaining decision is open-frame or inverter, and how much starting headroom you need. Open-frame units give more starting watts for the money, which matters for the sump pump and the RV air conditioner. Inverters are quieter and cleaner, which matters at a campground. Our <a href="/inverter-generator-vs-regular-generator/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">inverter vs regular generator comparison</a> covers that trade.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Planning Around the Starting Watts</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The starting figure lasts a second or two. A list that adds up to 4,000 watts will trip a generator rated 3,500 running and 4,375 starting within minutes. Plan around roughly 80 percent of the running watts, which is about 2,800 on most units in this class, and check starts separately.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running Two Heating Appliances at Once</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Coffee makers, microwaves, hair dryers, kettles and space heaters each draw 1,000 to 1,800 watts continuously. Two of them plus a refrigerator is past a 3,500 watt generator's running rating. Use one heating appliance at a time, and never while a big motor is starting.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the RV Fridge and Water Heater on Electric</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                On a 3,500 watt generator, the RV refrigerator and water heater element can use 1,700 watts or more between them, which is the headroom the air conditioner needs. Switch both to propane whenever the generator is your power source and the AC usually starts.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting It to Run a Small Well Pump</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Even a 1/2 HP well pump is usually 240V, and almost every 3,500 watt generator is 120V only. The watts look like they fit, but the voltage does not. If you have a well, you need a generator with a 120/240V L14-30R outlet.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using Long, Thin Extension Cords</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 100 foot 16 gauge cord drops enough voltage that motors draw more current to start, and a refrigerator that starts fine on a short cord can trip the generator on a long one. Use 12 gauge or heavier for motor loads, and keep runs as short as safe placement allows.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a 3500 watt generator run a house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It will run the essentials of a house with gas heat: refrigerator, freezer or furnace blower, lights, internet and small appliances one at a time. It will not run a house normally, because it is 120V only and too small for air conditioning, electric cooking, a water heater or a well pump. Treat it as a keep-the-food-cold-and-lights-on machine, not a whole-house one.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a 3500 watt generator run a refrigerator and freezer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. A refrigerator typically runs at about 700 watts and a chest freezer at about 500, so together they use well under half the running capacity. Their compressor starts, around 2,200 and 1,500 watts, fit the starting rating as long as both do not start in the same second. Plug them into different outlets and start them one after the other.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a 3500 watt generator run a 13,500 BTU RV air conditioner?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually, if it is an open-frame unit with around 4,375 or more starting watts and little else is running. Inverter models with lower surge ratings often trip, especially in heat or at altitude. A soft starter fitted to the AC cuts the starting surge enough that most 3,500 watt generators start it reliably. Keep the microwave off while the AC runs.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a 3500 watt generator run a window air conditioner?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                One small or mid-size unit, yes. A 10,000 BTU window AC typically runs at 1,000 to 1,200 watts and starts at up to about 3,000, so it fits alongside a refrigerator and lights but not alongside a refrigerator, freezer and sump pump. Start the AC first, then the refrigerator, and keep heating appliances off while it runs.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a 3500 watt generator run a well pump?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Almost never. Most residential well pumps are 240V, and almost every 3,500 watt generator is 120V only, so it cannot power them regardless of wattage. A few small 120V jet pumps exist, but their starting surge often exceeds what this class can deliver alongside other loads. Plan on a 5,000 watt or larger generator with an L14-30R outlet.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What does 3500 running watts mean?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It means the generator can deliver 3,500 watts continuously for hours. A generator listed as 3500 running watts is a slightly larger machine than one sold as 3500 watts peak, which may only run 3,000 continuously. Starting or surge watts are a separate, higher figure that only lasts a second or two while a motor starts.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long will a 3500 watt generator run on a tank of gas?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many open-frame models in this class are rated for somewhere around 10 to 14 hours at a quarter to half load on a full tank, and inverters can run longer at light load. At the 2,500 to 2,800 watt loads in this guide, expect noticeably less than the headline figure. Check the spec sheet's runtime and the load it was quoted at.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

